<h1>How to Migrate Old MD5 Password Hashes Safely</h1>
<p>Finding MD5 password hashes in an old application is common. Many systems were built when tutorials treated MD5 as acceptable password storage. Today, that design should be replaced. The challenge is that you usually do not know the user's original password, so you cannot immediately rehash every account with bcrypt or Argon2id.</p>
<p>A practical migration starts with inventory. Confirm the current format, database field length, salt behavior, and login code. A 32-character hexadecimal string often suggests MD5, but do not rely only on length. Check the code path that creates and verifies passwords. Also check admin reset tools, import scripts, mobile API endpoints, and old cron jobs.</p>
<p>The safest common approach is rehash-on-login. When a user logs in, verify the submitted password against the old MD5 format. If it matches, immediately hash the same submitted password with the new algorithm and replace the stored value. From that point on, verify the account with the new format. The user does not need to reset the password during that first migration step.</p>
<p>OWASP discusses upgrading legacy hashes in its <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>. The principle is to move toward modern password hashing without creating unnecessary plaintext handling. Do not export passwords, email passwords, or temporarily store plaintext values to make the migration easier. That would create a worse risk.</p>
<p>You also need a way to identify formats. Many modern password hash strings include algorithm markers and parameters. MD5 values may not. Add a password_algorithm column or store new hashes in a format that carries metadata. Your verification function can then route old MD5 hashes through the legacy check and new hashes through the modern verifier.</p>
<p>For inactive accounts, rehash-on-login may leave old hashes sitting in the database for a long time. Set a policy. You can force password resets after a deadline, disable long-inactive accounts, or require reset before the next login. The right choice depends on user expectations, business risk, and legal obligations.</p>
<p>Plan database changes carefully. Bcrypt and Argon2id hash strings are longer than MD5. A column sized only for 32 characters will not work. Increase the length before deployment, back up the database, test rollback paths, and monitor login errors after release. Password migrations should be boring, not improvised during a production incident.</p>
<p>Communication also matters. Users do not need to know every technical detail, but forced resets should be explained clearly if you choose that route. Avoid language that suggests passwords were exposed unless that is true. Say that the system is upgrading password protection and that some accounts must reset credentials to complete the change.</p>
<p>Do not forget backups and replicas. Old MD5 hashes may remain in database dumps, staging copies, analytics exports, or support tools after production has been upgraded. Include those locations in the cleanup plan, and restrict access to historic dumps that still contain weak password verifiers.</p>
<p>The final goal is not simply to remove MD5. The goal is to build a maintainable password system: modern algorithm, unique salts, adjustable cost, metadata in the stored hash, reset flow, rate limiting, and clear monitoring. Start with <a href="/why-md5-is-bad-for-passwords.html">Why MD5 Is Bad for Passwords</a> if you need to explain the business case before scheduling the work.</p>
